"The postoperative pathology reveals a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) diagnosis with pathological stage pT3a, involving extraprostatic extension and positive surgical margins. A reassuring finding is that the seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. Biopsy findings showed Gleason 3+3 in the peripheral zone and left base, with no evidence of cribriform pattern or intraductal carcinoma. The absence of documented nodal sampling means that the pNx status does not establish node-negative disease, which widens the uncertainty regarding regional spread. The positive margins and T3a extension are adverse prognostic factors. Postoperative PSA is not recorded, so biochemical recurrence cannot be assessed. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory would be crucial in revising the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 45.96 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 27.58–64.35 months; its coverage has not been validated."
